@page "/docs/components/tab"

<Seo Canonical="/docs/components/tab" Title="Blazorise Tab component" Description="Organize related content into tabbed panels with the Blazorise Tabs component. Learn how to connect tabs to panels and support accessible keyboard navigation." />

<DocsPageTitle Path="Components/Tab">
    Blazorise Tab component
</DocsPageTitle>

<DocsPageLead>
    Tabs organize related content into panels, allowing users to switch between them without leaving the page.
</DocsPageLead>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Use tabs for related sections that do not need to appear together, such as account settings.
    The <Code Tag>Tabs</Code> component manages selection and shows one panel at a time:
</DocsPageParagraph>

<UnorderedList>
    <UnorderedListItem>
        <Paragraph>
            <Code Tag>Items</Code> contains the <Code Tag>Tab</Code> components that users select to switch panels.
        </Paragraph>
    </UnorderedListItem>
    <UnorderedListItem>
        <Paragraph>
            <Code Tag>Content</Code> contains the <Code Tag>TabPanel</Code> components that hold each tab's content.
        </Paragraph>
    </UnorderedListItem>
</UnorderedList>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Give each <Code>Tab</Code> and its <Code>TabPanel</Code> the same <Code>Name</Code>. Use <Code>SelectedTab</Code>
    to set the initial selection, or bind it to keep selection in sync with your application state.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Usually, tabs and panels belong together inside <Code>Tabs</Code>. To render panels elsewhere, use
    <Code Tag>TabsContent</Code> and bind its <Code>SelectedPanel</Code> to the same value as <Code>Tabs.SelectedTab</Code>.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSectionTitle>
    Accessible labels
</DocsPageSectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Name the tab list so screen reader users can identify its purpose. Set <Code>AriaLabelledBy</Code> on
    <Code>Tabs</Code> to a visible heading's ID, or use <Code>AriaLabel="Account settings"</Code> when there is no heading.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Each <Code>Tab</Code> uses its text as its accessible name. Choose distinct labels that describe the panels,
    or set the tab's <Code>AriaLabelledBy</Code> when another element provides its label.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Within <Code>Tabs</Code>, matching <Code>Name</Code> values automatically connect tabs and panels through
    <Code>aria-controls</Code> and <Code>aria-labelledby</Code>. Names must be unique within each group.
    IDs are generated as needed; set <Code>ElementId</Code> to supply your own.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    For a separate <Code>TabsContent</Code>, provide explicit ARIA relationships and accessible panel names,
    since automatic associations are scoped to the parent <Code>Tabs</Code>.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSectionTitle>
    Keyboard navigation
</DocsPageSectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    The tab list is a single keyboard stop, so users can reach the content without visiting every tab.
    Moving focus does not change selection. Keep the focus indicator visible to distinguish the focused tab from the selected tab.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<UnorderedList>
    <UnorderedListItem>
        <Kbd>Tab</Kbd> focuses the selected tab, then the next focusable element in document order, usually the active panel.
        <Kbd>Shift</Kbd> + <Kbd>Tab</Kbd> moves backwards.
    </UnorderedListItem>
    <UnorderedListItem>
        <Kbd>Left Arrow</Kbd> / <Kbd>Right Arrow</Kbd> focus adjacent horizontal tabs; <Kbd>Up Arrow</Kbd> / <Kbd>Down Arrow</Kbd>
        do the same for vertical tabs. Navigation wraps and skips disabled tabs.
    </UnorderedListItem>
    <UnorderedListItem>
        <Kbd>Home</Kbd> focuses the first enabled tab, and <Kbd>End</Kbd> focuses the last enabled tab.
    </UnorderedListItem>
    <UnorderedListItem>
        <Kbd>Enter</Kbd> or <Kbd>Space</Kbd> selects the focused tab and displays its panel.
    </UnorderedListItem>
</UnorderedList>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Screen readers may require interaction mode for these keys; their own reading commands remain available for exploring content.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageSectionTitle>
    Panel focus
</DocsPageSectionTitle>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    <Code>TabPanel</Code> defaults to <Code>Focusable="true"</Code>, giving the active panel <Code>tabindex="0"</Code>.
    This provides a keyboard stop for reading text and scrolling the panel.
</DocsPageParagraph>

<DocsPageParagraph>
    Set <Code>Focusable="false"</Code> to skip the panel itself when its content starts with a focusable control and
    the panel does not need keyboard scrolling. Controls inside remain reachable with <Kbd>Tab</Kbd>.
    Keep the default for panels with introductory text, no focusable controls, or their own scrolling.
</DocsPageParagraph>

